{"results":[{"grant_id":"360G-Wellcome-061206_Z_00_Z","data":{"id":"360G-Wellcome-061206_Z_00_Z","title":"Matrix metalloproteinases in palatogenesis.  ","Region":"South West","currency":"GBP","awardDate":"2000-10-18T00:00:00+00:00","Sponsor(s)":"Dr Jason Mansell, Prof Jonathan Sandy","Internal ID":"061206/Z/00/Z","description":"No description available","plannedDates":[{"endDate":"2001-09-30T00:00:00+00:00","startDate":"2000-10-01T00:00:00+00:00"}],"amountAwarded":37707,"Financial Year":"2000/01","Lead Applicant":"Mr N L Brown","grantProgramme":[{"title":"Entry Level Fellowships for Medical & Dental Graduates"}],"Applicant Surname":"Brown","Approval Committee":"Clinical Interest Group","fundingOrganization":[{"id":"GB-CHC-210183","name":"The Wellcome Trust"}],"recipientOrganization":[{"id":"360G-Wellcome-ORG:University-of-Bristol","name":"University of Bristol","addressCountry":"United Kingdom","addressLocality":"Bristol"}],"Has the grant transferred?":"No","Research conducted at multiple locations?":"No","Total amount including partnership funding":37707},"publisher":{"self":"https://api.threesixtygiving.org/api/v1/org/GB-CHC-210183/","org_id":"GB-CHC-210183"},"data_license":{"url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"}},{"grant_id":"360G-Wellcome-061207_B_00_A","data":{"id":"360G-Wellcome-061207_B_00_A","title":"The mechanisms of activation of transcription by RNA polymerase II in mammalian cells.  ","Region":"North West","currency":"GBP","awardDate":"2005-06-01T00:00:00+00:00","Sponsor(s)":"Prof Andrew Sharrocks","Internal ID":"061207/B/00/A","description":"The regulation of gene expression is central to many biological processes and is frequently dysfunctional in many disease states. The regulation of gene expression largely occurs at the level of transcription, which can be subject to either activation or repression. The general transcription factor TFIIB is a direct target of transcriptional activator proteins. Activator proteins can not only recruit TFIIB to a promoter, but also induce an essential conformation change in TFIIB. In addition, TFIIB plays several other roles in the transcription process including the recruitment of RNA polymerase II and the selection of the transcription initiation site. This study will explore both the general and activator-specific functions of TFIIB. Using TFIIB mutants we will identify cellular genes in a DNA microarray screen that are regulated by TFIIB conformation. This will reveal core promoters/transcriptional activators that are dependent on TFIIB conformation for activity that will be tested further in direct assays. The role of chromatin and also multiprotein complexes (holoenzyme) on activator-mediated recruitment/conformational alteration of TFIIB will be determined in vitro. In addition we will characterise further a nuclear kinase activity that can phosphorylate TFIIB and alter its conformation. We have identified a new promoter DNA element downstream of the TATA box that is directly contacted by TFIIB and is only present in selected promoters that are sensitive to transcription start site shift by specific TFIIB mutants. The interplay between these obseravations will be analysed further using both biochemical and structural techniques. Finally, we will determine how activators regulate the sequence-specific DNA binding function of TFIIB and the role of coactivators in this process. These studies will significantly impact on our understanding of the mechanisms of transcriptional activation in mammalian cells.","plannedDates":[{"endDate":"2010-08-31T00:00:00+00:00","startDate":"2005-09-01T00:00:00+00:00"}],"amountAwarded":1031614,"Financial Year":"2004/05","Lead Applicant":"Dr Paul Shore","grantProgramme":[{"title":"Project Grant"}],"Applicant Surname":"Shore","Approval Committee":"Basic Science Interview Committee","fundingOrganization":[{"id":"GB-CHC-210183","name":"The Wellcome Trust"}],"recipientOrganization":[{"id":"360G-Wellcome-ORG:University-of-Manchester","name":"University of Manchester","addressCountry":"United Kingdom","addressLocality":"Manchester"}],"Has the grant transferred?":"No","Research conducted at multiple locations?":"No","Total amount including partnership funding":1031614},"publisher":{"self":"https://api.threesixtygiving.org/api/v1/org/GB-CHC-210183/","org_id":"GB-CHC-210183"},"data_license":{"url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"}},{"grant_id":"360G-Wellcome-061230_Z_00_E","data":{"id":"360G-Wellcome-061230_Z_00_E","title":"Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine Trian in Adults: Blantyre, Malawi.  ","Region":"North West","currency":"GBP","awardDate":"2008-10-02T00:00:00+00:00","Internal ID":"061230/Z/00/E","description":"Invasive pneumococcal disease (IPD) is the only important HIV-related problem in sub-Saharan Africa which is potentially vaccine preventable. Conservative estimates suggest 1 million cases of adult IPD occur annually in this region as a consequence of HIV-infection. In our recent efficacy trial in Uganda, the 23-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPV) increased the risk of IPD/pneumonia. The efficacy and role of the newer conjugate pneumococcal vaccines (CPV) remains unknown. I aim to investigate potential mechanisms of vaccine failure and harm associated with the use of PPV, and assess the response to and efficacy of CPV in a randomised controlled trial. CPV will be used to probe for defects in serological response to polysaccharides in a) previous PPV vaccine recipients b) individuals with past IPD, to describe and categorise the prejudicial effects of exposure to vaccine-purified or natural polysaccharide. A double-blind, randomised placebo controlled trial of CPV as secondary prophylaxis to prevent recurrent IPD will be performed in HIV- infected Malawian adults. Proven efficacy of the vaccine as secondary prophylaxis will be a fair indicator of potential as primary prophylaxis. Initial studies of the B-cell mechanisms that underpin altered or deleterious serological response will commence with validation of appropriate and \"field- friendly\" methods for the isolation of polysaccharide responsive B-cells, for future molecular-based descriptions of B-cell populations and dynamics.","plannedDates":[{"endDate":"2008-12-31T00:00:00+00:00","startDate":"2008-05-01T00:00:00+00:00"}],"amountAwarded":18700,"Financial Year":"2008/09","Lead Applicant":"Prof Neil French","grantProgramme":[{"title":"Career Development Fellowship in Clinical Tropical Medicine"}],"Applicant Surname":"French","Approval Committee":"Immunology and Infectious Disease Funding Committee","fundingOrganization":[{"id":"GB-CHC-210183","name":"The Wellcome Trust"}],"recipientOrganization":[{"id":"360G-Wellcome-ORG:Liverpool-School-of-Tropical-Medicine","name":"Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine","addressCountry":"United Kingdom","addressLocality":"Liverpool"}],"Has the grant transferred?":"No","Research conducted at multiple locations?":"Yes","Total amount including partnership funding":18700},"publisher":{"self":"https://api.threesixtygiving.org/api/v1/org/GB-CHC-210183/","org_id":"GB-CHC-210183"},"data_license":{"url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)"}},{"grant_id":"360G-Wellcome-061230_Z_00_Z","data":{"id":"360G-Wellcome-061230_Z_00_Z","title":"An investigation of the humoral immune response to pneumococcal polysaccharides and the role of a conjugate pneumococcal vaccine in secondary prevention of invasive pneumococcal disease in HIV-infected African adults.  ","Region":"North West","currency":"GBP","awardDate":"2000-12-13T00:00:00+00:00","Sponsor(s)":"Prof David Lalloo, Prof Charles Gilks","Internal ID":"061230/Z/00/Z","description":"Invasive pneumococcal disease (IPD) is the only important HIV-related problem in sub-Saharan Africa which is potentially vaccine preventable. Conservative estimates suggest 1 million cases of adult IPD occur annually in this region as a consequence of HIV-infection. In our recent efficacy trial in Uganda, the 23-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPV) increased the risk of IPD/pneumonia. The efficacy and role of the newer conjugate pneumococcal vaccines (CPV) remains unknown. I aim to investigate potential mechanisms of vaccine failure and harm associated with the use of PPV, and assess the response to and efficacy of CPV in a randomised controlled trial. CPV will be used to probe for defects in serological response to polysaccharides in a) previous PPV vaccine recipients b) individuals with past IPD, to describe and categorise the prejudicial effects of exposure to vaccine-purified or natural polysaccharide. A double-blind, randomised placebo controlled trial of CPV as secondary prophylaxis to prevent recurrent IPD will be performed in HIV- infected Malawian adults. Proven efficacy of the vaccine as secondary prophylaxis will be a fair indicator of potential as primary prophylaxis. Initial studies of the B-cell mechanisms that underpin altered or deleterious serological response will commence with validation of appropriate and \"field- friendly\" methods for the isolation of polysaccharide responsive B-cells, for future molecular-based descriptions of B-cell populations and dynamics.","plannedDates":[{"endDate":"2008-04-30T00:00:00+00:00","startDate":"2001-06-01T00:00:00+00:00"}],"amountAwarded":1016994,"Financial Year":"2000/01","Lead Applicant":"Prof Neil French","grantProgramme":[{"title":"Career Development Fellowship in Clinical Tropical Medicine"}],"Applicant Surname":"French","Approval Committee":"Tropical Interest Group","fundingOrganization":[{"id":"GB-CHC-210183","name":"The Wellcome Trust"}],"recipientOrganization":[{"id":"360G-Wellcome-ORG:University-of-Liverpool","name":"University of Liverpool","addressCountry":"United Kingdom","addressLocality":"Liverpo